Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "offensive five-man lineup changes",
    "shot mix",
    "personnel effects",
    "lineup stability",
    "offensive lineup design"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to identify causal effect of offensive lineup on shot mix?",
    "What confounding factors affect lineup change studies?",
    "What is the required sample size to detect shot mix changes from lineup swaps?",
    "How to separate offensive and defensive influences on shot distribution?",
    "Can shot mix changes serve as a manipulation check for lineage effects?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF34",
      "status_as_recorded": "inconclusive",
      "finding": "Offenses attack the rim more when the opponent has no big on the floor: DRIVE +0.82pp (SE 0.25), POST_UP +0.43pp (SE 0.18), 3PA -1.79pp (SE 0.31), rim ≤4ft +0.12pp (t=+0.4), PPP +0.008 (t=+1.1). Sample N=1.07M possessions."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-TMP-009",
      "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
      "finding": "The PULL_UP-specific decline with exposure is defensive adaptation rather than shooter selection/lineup artifact. With lineup FE: slope holding shot fixed -0.0255 (0.0061); shooter's earlier scored -0.1044 vs not scored +0.0693. No lineup FE: -0.0063 (0.0036); placebo other play types -0.0075 (0.0017)."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF32",
      "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
      "finding": "Rank-1 openers are within what the top-8 rotation can sustain. 8/8 rank-1 openers CUT-centered and INFEASIBLE; worst gaps +9.6 to +15.9pp; CUT target 14-18% vs ceiling 2-6%."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-087",
      "status_as_recorded": "rejected",
      "finding": "Connector presence shields stars (higher TS, lower TO) and lifts lineup ORtg beyond talent. Star dTS +0.05 pp p=0.937; dTO +0.74 pp p=0.00364 (wrong sign). Star-init TO~HasConn beta=+0.34 pp p=0.439. HasConn on-off PPP BOTH_NULL (mean -0.041 p=0.939, median +0.528 p=0.731, n=73)."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-TMP-009",
      "what_failed": "Attempt to detect a defensive adaptation effect on pull-up shooting without accounting for shooter-level confound and lineup fixed effects.",
      "why": "C_artifact (apparent effect produced by a construction, selection or labelling flaw). Effect shrinks from -0.0255 to -0.0063 without lineup FE and the same-shooter placebo on other play types is -0.0071/-0.0075, consistent with shooter-level confound."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF32",
      "what_failed": "Optimizer recommendations that target specific play-type frequencies for opening lineups without considering personnel feasibility.",
      "why": "C_artifact (apparent effect produced by a construction, selection or labelling flaw). All rank-1 openers were CUT-centered and exceeded the stretch cap by +9.6 to +15.9 pp for every team."
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "Shot mix can shift detectably in response to lineup changes (even opponent lineup), but shifts are often small (FAM:SF34).",
    "Confounding by shooter talent and game context can create spurious lineup effects on shot selection; including lineup fixed effects reduces bias but does not eliminate it (FAM:SF-TMP-009).",
    "Apparent lineup-level effects on performance metrics can be due to talent confounds rather than a causal effect of the lineup composition (FAM:SF-APPS-087).",
    "Optimization targets based purely on shot-mix objectives can be infeasible when personnel constraints are ignored (FAM:SF32)."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "No study tested the specific hypothesis that changing the offensive five causes a change in the offense's shot mix, holding other factors constant.",
    "The studies did not use the same 2022-25 NBA seasons or the same data pipeline.",
    "The effect sizes for shot mix changes induced by own-lineup changes are unknown; the historical evidence only covers opponent-driven changes and confounded observational designs.",
    "Whether a pre-registered quasi-experimental design can overcome endogeneity and detect a meaningful signal remains untested."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The question may be trivially true (different players take different shots), so the meaningful challenge is to detect a causal signal above noise and confounds.",
    "A naive pre-post substitution comparison of shot mix will likely produce artifacts, as shown by the pull-up and connector studies.",
    "A design that uses player fixed effects, opponent lineup controls, and a pre-specified composite shot mix index can mitigate confounding but still faces risks from unobserved time-varying confounders.",
    "The large sample of three seasons and detailed substitution data make it feasible to power the study if effect sizes are similar to the −1.8 pp 3PA shift observed in FAM:SF34."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The specific design intended by the researchers (randomization, optimization, or natural experiment).",
    "The operational definition of shot mix (which shot types, at what granularity) and how personnel effects will be quantified.",
    "The expected magnitude of the effect; without a prior estimate power calculations are speculative.",
    "Whether shifts in shot mix due to own-lineup changes are larger or smaller than those induced by opponent adjustments."
  ],
